  What changed along the way:
  - [1mNo [38;5;153muserId[39m parameter.[0m I didn't add the parameter you asked for, because nothing has a real user ID before login and
    any value passed in could be faked. With your OK, [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m keeps its inputs and returns [38;5;153muserId[39m
    instead.
  - [1mBigger scope than first planned.[0m Once you said other forms will need the user later, this became a shared piece of
    the app rather than a one-function edit. So I wrote a full spec instead of a short in-chat design.

  The spec covers:
  - [1mComponents:[0m a new [38;5;153msession.js[39m exposing a [38;5;153mSession[39m object backed by [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m. It has [38;5;153msetCurrentUser[39m,
    [38;5;153mgetCurrentUser[39m, [38;5;153mgetCurrentUserId[39m and [38;5;153mclearSession[39m.
  - [1mChanges to [38;5;153mapp.js[39m:[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns a stand-in [38;5;153muserId[39m until the real API exists, and the user is saved only after a
    successful login. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153msession.js[39m first.
  - [1mError handling:[0m a storage failure logs a warning and gets treated as "no user"; a corrupt saved value gets cleared;
    a missing [38;5;153muserId[39m throws; a failed login doesn't log out whoever was already logged in. A comment marks the stored ID
    as browser-only — servers must check identity themselves.
  - [1mTesting:[0m [38;5;153mnpm test[39m runs Node's built-in test runner on [38;5;153mtests/session.test.js[39m and [38;5;153mtests/app.test.js[39m, with no new
    dependencies, plus a manual check in the browser.
  - [1mOut of scope:[0m logout UI, session expiry, sharing across tabs, a real backend, linting and browser-automation tests.

